/*
 * LC 1 — Two Sum
 *
 * PROBLEM:
 * Given integer array nums and integer target, return indices of
 * two numbers that add up to target. Exactly one solution guaranteed.
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - 2 <= nums.length <= 10^4   → always at least one pair
 * - -10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9  → negatives possible
 * - Exactly one valid solution → no need to handle multiple answers
 * - Cannot use same element twice → i != j
 * - Return indices in any order
 *
 * ALL APPROACHES:
 * ┌─────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬───────┬──────┐
 * │ Approach    │ Idea                             │ TC    │ SC   │
 * ├─────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼───────┼──────┤
 * │ Brute Force │ Every pair, check sum == target  │ O(n²) │ O(1) │
 * │ HashMap     │ Complement lookup                │ O(n)  │ O(n) │
 * └─────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴───────┴──────┘
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 1 — BRUTE FORCE
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY: Simplest — check every possible pair.
 * HOW: Two nested loops. For every pair (i,j) where j > i,
 *      check if nums[i] + nums[j] == target → return {i, j}.
 *
 * REJECTED BECAUSE:
 * O(n²) — for n=10^4 that's 10^8 operations. TLE risk.
 *
 * TC: O(n²) — n*(n-1)/2 pairs checked worst case
 * SC: O(1)  — no extra space
 */
public int[] twoSumBrute(int[] nums, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        // j starts at i+1 — avoid using same element twice
        for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {
            if (nums[i] + nums[j] == target) {
                return new int[]{i, j};
            }
        }
    }
    // guaranteed solution exists — never reaches here
    return new int[]{};
}

/*
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 2 — HASHMAP (Optimal)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY HashMap:
 * For each nums[i], we need to know if (target - nums[i]) exists.
 * That is a complement lookup — classic HashMap pattern.
 * Store value → index mapping for O(1) retrieval.
 *
 * HOW:
 * Single pass. For each index i:
 *   complement = target - nums[i]
 *   if map.containsKey(complement) → found pair → return indices
 *   else → map.put(nums[i], i) and continue
 *
 * CRITICAL — CHECK FIRST, PUT AFTER:
 * Must check complement BEFORE putting current element.
 * If put first:
 *   nums=[3,3], target=6
 *   i=0: put(3,0) → map:{3→0}
 *   i=1: put(3,1) → map:{3→1} ← overwrites index 0!
 *        complement=3 found at index 1 → return [1,1] ❌ same index twice!
 * Check first:
 *   i=0: complement=3? map empty → NO → put(3,0)
 *   i=1: complement=3? YES at index 0 → return [0,1] ✅
 *
 * WHY store value as key, index as value:
 * We search by value (complement lookup) → value must be key for O(1)
 * We return indices → index must be stored as value
 * containsValue() would be O(n) → wrong choice here
 *
 * TC: O(n)  — single pass, O(1) per containsKey/put
 * SC: O(n)  — worst case all elements stored before pair found
 *
 * Best case:  O(1) — complement found at index 1
 * Worst case: O(n) — pair at last two indices
 *
 * Edge cases:
 * - nums=[3,3], target=6 → handled by check-first pattern ✅
 * - Negatives → HashMap handles any Integer value ✅
 * - Guaranteed one solution → no need for not-found handling ✅
 */
public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
    Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        int complement = target - nums[i];
        // check first — avoid using same index twice
        if (map.containsKey(complement)) {
            return new int[]{map.get(complement), i};
        }
        // put after — only store current element after checking
        map.put(nums[i], i);
    }
    // guaranteed solution exists — never reaches here
    return new int[]{};
}
